One thing to keep in mind with C++ is that the language standard has evolved heavily over the years. The constexpr system in particular has gotten a ton of attention and features since many of these projects were first started. There's an entire section in the core C++ guidelines about avoiding the use of macros.

https://isocpp.github.io/CppCoreGuidelines/CppCoreGuidelines#es33-if-you-must-use-macros-give-them-unique-names

> Avoid macros if you can: [ES.30](https://isocpp.github.io/CppCoreGuidelines/CppCoreGuidelines#res-macros), [ES.31](https://isocpp.github.io/CppCoreGuidelines/CppCoreGuidelines#res-macros2), and [ES.32](https://isocpp.github.io/CppCoreGuidelines/CppCoreGuidelines#res-all_caps). However, there are billions of lines of code littered with macros and a long tradition for using and overusing macros. ...

We are introducing a greenfield C++20 implementation with this PR, and have to care very little about providing an API for third party libraries to use. Our use case is different than Arrow/Highway which both started on older C++ standards, and have to support third parties, so we can be aggressive about using newer standards as long as our CI supports them

